<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zipporah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zipporah appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> as the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and a daughter of Jethro, the priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midian">Midian</a>. According to Exodus, Moses defended Jethro's daughters at a well in Midian, and Jethro gave her to him. She bore two sons, Gershom and Eliezer. In an obscure passage at an inn on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, she circumcises her son and God spares Moses; the details are debated. Later, Jethro brings her and her sons to Moses in the wilderness of Sinai. Numbers 12 mentions a Cushite wife of Moses, and interpreters differ on whether this is Zipporah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zipporah.jpg" alt="Zipporah" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Ruskin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zipporah_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zipporah is said to have been one of the seven daughters of Jethro, a Kenite shepherd who was priest and prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midian">Midian</a>. Exodus 2:18 also calls him Reuel, and Judges 4:11 calls him Hobab, a name that Numbers 10:29 gives to Jethro's son. In the Book of Chronicles two of her grandsons are mentioned: Shebuel, son of Gershom, and Rehabiah, son of Eliezer.</p><p>In the narrative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> had killed an Egyptian who was striking a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midian">Midian</a>. At a well he drove off shepherds who had pushed Jethro's daughters away from the water and watered their flocks. When the girls told their father that an Egyptian had rescued them, Jethro asked why they had left the man and invited him to supper. He then gave Zipporah to Moses as his wife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When God commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Moses set out with his wife and sons. At an inn on the road God came to kill Moses. Zipporah quickly circumcised her son with a sharp stone, touched Moses's feet with the foreskin and said, according to Exodus 4:24–26, that he was a husband of blood to her, and God left Moses alone.</p><p>After the exodus and a victory over Amalek, Jethro came to the camp in the wilderness of Sinai, bringing Zipporah and their two sons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> does not say when she and her sons had gone back to Jethro, only that he brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>'s family to him. The most common translation is that Moses sent her away, but another grammatically permissible reading is that she sent things or persons, perhaps news of the victory; the difficulty comes from the word shelucheiha in Exodus 18:2.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Numbers 12 speaks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>'s wife as a Cushite woman, and interpreters differ on whether she was Zipporah or another woman, and whether the marriages were simultaneous or successive. Jewish reading tradition pronounces the word as kushit, while the Samaritan Pentateuch's oral tradition reads kaashet, the beautiful woman. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> renders it Αἰθιόπισσα, and the 17th-century Jesuit Alonso de Sandoval held that Zipporah and the Cushite woman were the same person and was black.</p><p>In Islamic tradition she is Safura, whose story is drawn from Surah Al-Qasas, and a son Gershom is mentioned. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> religion Jethro is revered as the founder and chief prophet, and some Druze say that this relates them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> through marriage. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcel-proust">Marcel Proust</a>'s Swann's Way, Swann is struck by the resemblance of Odette to Botticelli's painting of Zipporah in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zipporah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
